# Wiring your existing SonarQube install to this bundle

You generated this bundle with `--edition none`, so it does not run SonarQube — it assumes you
already have a zip/host install of SonarQube running, and only starts the containers around it
(database is external in this mode too). These are the changes to make in *your* SonarQube's
`sonar.properties`.

## 1. Database

This bundle points the containers at the same external database you passed to `generate.py`.
Point your SonarQube at it too, if it isn't already:

```
sonar.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://${db_host}:${db_port}/${db_name}${db_url_params}
sonar.jdbc.username=${db_user}
sonar.jdbc.password=<the --db-password you passed to generate.py>
```

The orchestrator connects with the same `sslmode=verify-full`: the database must serve TLS with a
certificate valid for `${db_host}`. Your SonarQube validates it against its JVM truststore, so the
CA behind it must be in the JDK's default `cacerts` or in the `-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore` you set
in `sonar.web.javaAdditionalOpts` and `sonar.ce.javaAdditionalOpts`.

